1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,104
This is the go-to-market plan for SchoolNossa, from November 2026 to May 2027.

2
00:00:06,154 --> 00:00:09,166
It runs on Instagram, Facebook and YouTube.

3
00:00:09,216 --> 00:00:12,726
It costs at most one hundred euros a month in ads.

4
00:00:12,776 --> 00:00:18,351
And it is built so that Claude runs it, with about twenty minutes of your time.

5
00:00:18,401 --> 00:00:21,270
No spam, and no emails to existing users.

6
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:23,672
Here is where we stand.

7
00:00:23,722 --> 00:00:27,200
One hundred and nine website visitors in three months.

8
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:28,441
Fifty-one accounts.

9
00:00:28,491 --> 00:00:35,234
Four hundred and fifty-two of the five hundred free beta places are still open, and nobody has paid yet.

10
00:00:35,284 --> 00:00:39,807
The data, the website, the iPhone app and the AI connectors are ready.

11
00:00:39,857 --> 00:00:43,661
The problem is that almost nobody knows SchoolNossa exists.

12
00:00:43,711 --> 00:00:45,490
So this plan is about reach.

13
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:53,119
In May we spent two hundred and forty euros in one week, on Meta and Google, across fourteen cities.

14
00:00:53,169 --> 00:00:57,492
It felt expensive and converted badly, and the reasons are clear.

15
00:00:57,542 --> 00:00:58,703
May is off-season.

16
00:00:58,753 --> 00:01:00,722
The money was spread too thin.

17
00:01:00,772 --> 00:01:03,279
The message gave no reason to act now.

18
00:01:03,329 --> 00:01:08,460
Every click landed on the homepage, where visitors can't see any school data.

19
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:10,277
And we only counted clicks.

20
00:01:10,327 --> 00:01:12,430
This plan changes each of these.

21
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:17,798
The idea in one sentence: be there when parents have to choose.

22
00:01:17,848 --> 00:01:24,677
Every city has fixed dates for open days and for registration, and we have them for every school.

23
00:01:24,727 --> 00:01:30,917
So we show up in one city, in exactly those weeks, with information parents need anyway.

24
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:32,690
That is useful, not spam.

25
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:34,950
This is the calendar.

26
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,908
Each light blue bar is an ad flight, and the orange mark is that city's registration window.

27
00:01:40,958 --> 00:01:46,024
Hamburg and Berlin start this month, because their open days are happening now.

28
00:01:46,074 --> 00:01:56,645
Then come Bremen, Düsseldorf and Köln, Dresden and Leipzig in February, Berlin, Frankfurt and Stuttgart in early March, the Netherlands in March, and Munich in May.

29
00:01:56,695 --> 00:01:59,430
The money always follows the next deadline.

30
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:02,993
Three channels, each with one job.

31
00:02:03,043 --> 00:02:07,536
Meta ads buy reach in one city, right before its deadline.

32
00:02:07,586 --> 00:02:18,346
Instagram and Facebook posts are the free, steady part: open days this week, deadline reminders, and how school choice works in each city.

33
00:02:18,396 --> 00:02:25,082
YouTube answers what parents type into search, like "Anmeldung Gymnasium Berlin 2027".

34
00:02:25,132 --> 00:02:30,330
Few videos answer that city by city, so that is our free advantage.

35
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:34,941
Here is what parents will actually see, with real data.

36
00:02:34,991 --> 00:02:40,811
A weekly list of open days in their city, for the two cities with the nearest deadline.

37
00:02:40,861 --> 00:02:45,263
A deadline card, fourteen days and three days before registration.

38
00:02:45,313 --> 00:02:51,538
A short explainer of how school choice works, taken from the official rules, with the source.

39
00:02:51,588 --> 00:02:55,249
And every two weeks, a demo of one SchoolNossa feature.

40
00:02:55,299 --> 00:02:58,150
Every post is useful even if nobody clicks.

41
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:01,944
This is the path from a post to an account.

42
00:03:01,994 --> 00:03:05,039
Every ad, post and video links to a city page.

43
00:03:05,089 --> 00:03:11,364
That page shows the open days of the next weeks and the official deadline, without an account.

44
00:03:11,414 --> 00:03:16,411
Then comes the offer: the first five hundred testers get three months free.

45
00:03:16,461 --> 00:03:21,593
The link carries a short campaign code, and it is saved with the new account.

46
00:03:21,643 --> 00:03:27,515
So we know which ad brought which account, without a tracking pixel and without cookies.

47
00:03:27,565 --> 00:03:30,610
Every ad is judged by accounts, not by clicks.

48
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:33,851
The budget follows the same calendar.

49
00:03:33,901 --> 00:03:39,609
Fifty euros for the rest of November, for Berlin and Hamburg, while we test three messages.

50
00:03:39,659 --> 00:03:45,177
Then a hundred euros a month, always split across the cities with the nearest deadlines.

51
00:03:45,227 --> 00:03:50,429
In the summer it drops to thirty to fifty euros, for families moving to a new city.

52
00:03:50,479 --> 00:03:59,350
Ads run only on Instagram and Facebook, in the city plus fifteen kilometres, for ages twenty-eight to fifty-five, at about three euros a day.

53
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:03,900
No spam is enforced by rules, not by good intentions.

54
00:04:03,950 --> 00:04:09,065
At most three posts a week, one story a day, and one YouTube upload a week.

55
00:04:09,115 --> 00:04:14,299
An ad pauses as soon as people see it more than two and a half times a week.

56
00:04:14,349 --> 00:04:16,985
The spending cap is set in Meta itself.

57
00:04:17,035 --> 00:04:19,808
And some things are impossible by design.

58
00:04:19,858 --> 00:04:28,141
The access key has no messaging permission, so it cannot send DMs or write comments, and it cannot post in parent groups.

59
00:04:28,191 --> 00:04:34,890
We never upload user emails to Meta, we never email users, and we don't retarget website visitors.

60
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,732
Here is how it runs without you.

61
00:04:37,782 --> 00:04:42,343
Once a week, on Monday morning, a Claude task on your Mac does the thinking.

62
00:04:42,393 --> 00:04:57,025
It reads the stats and the new accounts, writes the next two weeks of posts from our data, checks every date and number against the database, renders the images and videos with a voice-over, sets up the next ads and uploads the YouTube video.

63
00:04:57,075 --> 00:04:59,634
The posts wait in a queue, two weeks ahead.

64
00:04:59,684 --> 00:05:06,248
Every day, a small cloud job publishes the posts that are due and pauses weak ads, even when your Mac is off.

65
00:05:06,298 --> 00:05:09,888
Meta's own spending limit sits underneath as the safety net.

66
00:05:09,938 --> 00:05:15,470
You get two lines in the morning email you already receive, and a one-page report each week.

67
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:18,180
A normal week looks like this.

68
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:24,937
On Monday, Claude plans, writes, checks and renders, sets up the ads, uploads to YouTube and writes your report.

69
00:05:24,987 --> 00:05:30,064
On Tuesday, the week's open days go out for the two cities with the nearest deadline.

70
00:05:30,114 --> 00:05:33,020
On Thursday, a deadline card or a city explainer.

71
00:05:33,070 --> 00:05:37,666
On Saturday, a feature demo or an explainer, and the YouTube Short goes live.

72
00:05:37,716 --> 00:05:42,130
Every day, the ad rules run, and the numbers appear in your morning email.

73
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:43,865
What you do.

74
00:05:43,915 --> 00:05:45,744
Once, about twenty minutes.

75
00:05:45,794 --> 00:05:50,058
Create the Instagram account and link it to the Facebook Page.

76
00:05:50,108 --> 00:05:51,937
Create the YouTube channel.

77
00:05:51,987 --> 00:05:56,251
Press Generate token in Meta, and paste the key into one file.

78
00:05:56,301 --> 00:06:00,914
And say yes to the budget cap, the city pages and six sample posts.

79
00:06:00,964 --> 00:06:07,246
These are the only steps I'm not allowed to do: creating accounts and handling access keys.

80
00:06:07,296 --> 00:06:09,473
After that, nothing is required.

81
00:06:09,523 --> 00:06:16,710
I only come back to you for real decisions, and you can say pause in any Claude chat to stop everything.

82
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:19,072
What success looks like.

83
00:06:19,122 --> 00:06:22,057
Our target is five euros or less per new account.

84
00:06:22,107 --> 00:06:30,402
With a hundred euros we expect roughly six to thirty-three new accounts a month from ads, plus whatever the free posts and YouTube bring.

85
00:06:30,452 --> 00:06:34,117
These are estimates; after two weeks we'll have real numbers.

86
00:06:34,167 --> 00:06:37,407
On the thirty-first of December there is a checkpoint.

87
00:06:37,457 --> 00:06:45,630
If an account costs more than ten euros after a hundred and fifty euros spent, the ads stop by themselves, and the free posts continue.

88
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:46,970
You can overrule that.

89
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:50,572
What could go wrong, and what happens then.

90
00:06:50,622 --> 00:06:56,731
If your Mac is off on Monday, the queue still covers two weeks, and the ad rules run in the cloud.

91
00:06:56,781 --> 00:06:59,748
If Meta rejects an ad, the next variant goes in.

92
00:06:59,798 --> 00:07:05,970
If the access key stops working, you get a failure email the same morning, and the caps still hold.

93
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:10,055
A wrong date can't get through, because the fact check blocks it.

94
00:07:10,105 --> 00:07:15,523
Reach will start small, which is why the ads and YouTube search carry the first months.

95
00:07:15,573 --> 00:07:23,190
And because YouTube locks uploads from new API projects, I upload through YouTube Studio in Chrome until the audit passes.

96
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:24,810
Next steps.

97
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:28,935
This week, you do the twenty-minute setup and give three yeses.

98
00:07:28,985 --> 00:07:34,108
I build the city pages, the campaign codes, the automation and the first posts.

99
00:07:34,158 --> 00:07:41,377
The first posts go out on the sixteenth of November, and the first ads in Berlin and Hamburg on the eighteenth.

100
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:45,043
On the thirty-first of December, we look at the numbers.

101
00:07:45,093 --> 00:07:52,770
To start, I need three yeses: the hundred-euro monthly cap, the public open-day pages, and the first six sample posts.
